Q: How to strike through obliquely with css A: <p>Based on the SVG version of Martin, but embedded as the css background.<br />
As stated in his answer, the &quot;non-scaling-stroke&quot; is the key.</p>
<pre><code>
.strikethrough {
  position: relative;
}

.strikethrough::after {
  position: absolute;
  left: 0;
  top: 0;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  content: &quot;&quot;;
  background-image: url(&quot;data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg viewBox='0 0 1 1' preserveAspectRatio='none' fill='none' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%3E%3Cpath d='M0 1 L1 0' stroke='black' stroke-width='5' vector-effect='non-scaling-stroke'/%3E%3C/svg%3E&quot;);
  background-size: 100% 100%;
}
</code></pre>

Q: LibGDX autoscale text in Label A: <p>I never tried it, but if you look to the <a href="https://libgdx.badlogicgames.com/nightlies/docs/api/com/badlogic/gdx/scenes/scene2d/ui/Label.html" rel="nofollow">Label documentation</a>, there is a <a href="https://libgdx.badlogicgames.com/nightlies/docs/api/com/badlogic/gdx/scenes/scene2d/ui/Label.html#setFontScale-float-" rel="nofollow">setFontScale method</a>.</p>

<p>Did you try it ?</p>

<p>The width of the label is given by <code>yourLabel.getPrefWidth()</code> and the width of the text inside the label is given by <code>yourLabel.getGlyphLayout.width</code>. You could compare the both, and scale down the font if the width of the text is bigger thant the width of the label.</p>

<p>I guess you could use something like this in your <code>render()</code> :</p>

<pre><code>if(yourLabel.getGlyphLayout.width &gt; yourLabel.getPrefWidth()){
    yourLabel.setFontScale(yourLabel.getPrefWidth()/yourLabel.getGlyphLayout.width);
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to copy a p5.js array of Loaded Images? A: <p>Browser caching should make this a non-issue. When the code requests an image in the same script, the browser quickly retrieves the image from its cache, either without a network request, or with a quick 304 from the server instructing the client to use its cache.</p>
<p>As such, if your real goal is to optimize a slow-loading script, I suggest providing full problem context (complete, runnable code). It's possible that the images themselves are unoptimized, or that something other than image loading is the bottleneck.</p>
<p>That said, you can use <code>.get()</code> to make a copy, although my suspicion is that this won't actually speed things up as you expect:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>const originals = [];
const copies = [];
const urls = [
  "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3c/Shaki_waterfall.jpg",
  "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e1/Cattle_tyrant_%28Machetornis_rixosa%29_on_Capybara.jpg",
  "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/02/Capybara.jpg",
];

function preload() {
  for (const url of urls) {
    originals.push(loadImage(url));
  }
}

function setup() {
  createCanvas(900, 400);

  // prove that they're actually copies by modifying pixels
  for (let i = 0; i &lt; originals.length; i++) {
    const img = originals[i].get();
    img.loadPixels();

    for (let p = 0; p &lt; img.pixels.length; p += 4) {
      img.pixels[p] = i * 120;
    }

    img.updatePixels();
    copies.push(img);
  }

  noLoop();
  background(220);
  const w = width / originals.length;

  for (const [i, original] of originals.entries()) {
    image(original, i * w, 0, w, height / 2);
    image(copies[i], i * w, height / 2, w, height / 2);
  }
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/p5.js/1.11.0/p5.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
